WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.000
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.450
Everyone who watches this series knows that
among many other scientific topics, I am severely

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:15.139
addicted to fluid simulations, and today,
it's time to relapse!

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:19.400
And this time, we're going to run wind tunnel
tests on hummingbirds.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.019
Typically, when a new engine, airplane, or
even a new phone is being designed, we're

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:29.880
interested in knowing how the heat flow and
dissipation will look like, preferably before

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.730
we're designing an object.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.470
To do so, we often run some virtual wind tunnel
tests and optimize our design until we're

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.880
happy with the results.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.370
Then, we can proceed to build these new contraptions.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:48.560
Simulating the pressure distribution and aerodynamic
forces is a large topic, however, visualizing

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.740
these results is at least as well-studied
and difficult as writing a simulator.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.060
What is it exactly that we're interested in?

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.950
Even if we have an intuitive particle-based
simulation, millions and millions of particles,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.370
it is clearly impossible to show the path
for every one of them.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.480
Grid-based simulations are often even more
challenging to visualize well.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.960
So how do we choose what to visualize and
what not to show on the screen?

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:19.930
And in this paper, we can witness a new way
of visualizing velocity and vorticity fields.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.409
And this visualization happens through Clebsch-maps.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:28.909
This is a mathematical transformation where
we create a sphere, and a set of points on

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:34.020
this sphere correspond to vortex lines and
their evolution over time.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.730
However, if instead of only points, we pick
an entire region on this sphere, as you can

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:45.549
see the north and south pole regions here,
we obtain vortex tubes.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:52.200
These vortex tubes provide an accurate representation
of the vorticity information within the simulation,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:58.060
and this is one of the rare cases where the
validity of such a solution can also be shown.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.960
Such a crazy idea, loving it!

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.980
And with this, we can get a better understanding
of the air flow around the wings of a hummingbird,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:11.569
but we can also learn more from pre-existing
NASA aircraft datasets.

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:14.340
Have a look at these incredible results.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.219
Publishing a paper at the SIGGRAPH conference
is an incredible feat that typically takes

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:24.120
a few brilliant guys and several years of
unbelievably hard work.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.549
Well, apparently this is not such a challenge
for Albert Chern, who was also the first author

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:34.849
of this and the Schrödinger's Smoke paper
just a year ago that we reported on.

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:40.439
He is doing incredible work at taking a piece
of mathematical theory and showing remarkable

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:45.489
applications of it in new areas where we would
think it doesn't belong at all.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:50.129
The link is available in the video description,
for both this and the previous works, make

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:51.129
sure to have a look.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:56.400
There is lots of beautifully written mathematics
to be read there that seems to be from another

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.400
world.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.209
It's a truly unique experience.

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:04.059
The paper reports that the source code is
also available, but I was unable to find it

00:03:04.059 --> 00:03:05.059
yet.

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:08.549
If you have found a public implementation,
please let me know and I'll update the video

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:09.909
description with your link.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time.

